---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
---
<Base title="Current law in India" description="What Indian law actually says about e-cigarettes and smokeless nicotine products, as of today." wide={true}>
  <section class="breakout law-hero">
    <div class="breakout-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">As of today</p>
      <h1>The law in India, as it stands</h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="law-body">
    <div class="status-grid">
      <div class="status-card status-banned">
        <Icon name="shield" />
        <h3>E-cigarettes &amp; ENDS</h3>
        <p class="status-tag">Sale banned — use is not</p>
        <p>PECA, 2019 bans production, sale, import and advertisement outright. Personal use and possession are <a href="#know-your-rights">not banned</a> — PECA has no provision that makes either an offence.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="status-card status-blocked">
        <Icon name="leaf" />
        <h3>Nicotine pouches &amp; snus</h3>
        <p class="status-tag">No lawful pathway</p>
        <p>Not named in any ban, but blocked by two separate mechanisms — no FSSAI approval to sell, and import treated as case-by-case discretionary refusal.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="status-card status-legal">
        <Icon name="heart" />
        <h3>NRT (gums, patches)</h3>
        <p class="status-tag">Legal &amp; regulated</p>
        <p>Regulated as medicines under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940, and actively supported by government as a cessation aid.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="law-prose">
      <h2><Icon name="shield" /> E-cigarettes and ENDS</h2>
      <p>
        Electronic cigarettes are banned in India under the <a href="https://www.indiacode.nic.in/handle/123456789/13078" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prohibition of Electronic Cigarettes Act, 2019 (PECA)</a>,
        Act No. 42 of 2019, passed by Parliament on 5 December 2019 (it repealed
        an earlier ordinance of the same name). The Act prohibits the
        production, manufacture, import, export, transport, sale, distribution,
        storage and advertisement of e-cigarettes and all Electronic Nicotine
        Delivery Systems (ENDS), including heat-not-burn products and e-hookah
        — in short, it bans the <strong>sale and commercialisation</strong> of
        these products. It does <strong>not</strong> ban personal use or
        possession: no provision anywhere in PECA makes vaping, or simply
        carrying an e-cigarette for your own use, an offence. See <a href="#know-your-rights">"Know your rights" below</a> if you've
        been told otherwise.
      </p>
      <p>
        First-offence penalties under PECA — imprisonment of up to one year
        and a fine of up to ₹1 lakh, with repeat offences carrying higher
        penalties — attach to the <em>commercial</em> acts the Act actually
        lists: production, sale, storage for sale, transport for sale and
        advertisement. In late 2023, the Union Health Ministry filed an
        opinion in a court case brought by AVI members challenging India's
        e-cigarette flight-ban rules, arguing that because sale and import
        are banned, this <em>implies</em> use is banned too. That isn't how
        Indian law works: making an act an offence requires a specific
        provision creating it and a specified penalty for it, and neither
        exists for personal use or possession anywhere in PECA. In fact, the
        then Union Health Minister, Dr Harsh Vardhan, <a href="https://twitter.com/vapeindia/status/1200639227425366017?t=UZhVQ7iMF4hDjVOPfSiiZw&s=19" target="_blank" rel="noopener">assured Parliament</a> during the debate on the ENDS prohibition bill that use and possession
        would not be banned, since doing so would violate personal liberties —
        which is exactly why PECA, as finally passed, doesn't mention personal
        use anywhere in its text. It's also a reversal of the Ministry's own
        earlier position: its <a href="https://twitter.com/MoHFW_INDIA/status/1174325122993278977?t=6X-kwOEHGvSL3ndHKTj9uQ&s=19" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official Twitter account had stated</a>,
        months before PECA was even passed, that use and possession are not
        banned. The Union Home Ministry has separately issued a <a href="https://www.mha.gov.in/sites/default/files/CSDiv_ecigerete_12122019.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">circular (see point C)</a> confirming that personal use and possession is not a crime, and
        independent legal experts have reached the <a href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ7MCllo4KJ/?igsh=MXc2aHZmMW53Zm5lMg==" target="_blank" rel="noopener">same conclusion</a>.
        See <a href="/litigation">AVI's litigation page</a> for the ongoing
        flight-ban case, and our <a href="https://x.com/vapeindia/status/1848254084572172776?s=20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">full commentary</a> on this issue.
      </p>

    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="breakout rights-section" id="know-your-rights">
    <div class="breakout-inner">
      <div class="rights-content">
        <p class="rights-eyebrow"><Icon name="scale" /> Know your rights</p>
        <h2>Vaping in public? Use and possession are not banned.</h2>
        <p class="rights-lede">
          Police harassment of vapers — stopping people on the street,
          claiming e-cigarettes are "completely illegal," and demanding cash
          "fines" — is a real and recurring problem in India, and it rests
          on a misunderstanding (or a deliberate one) of what PECA actually
          says. This section exists as a citable, definitive reference:
          bookmark it, screenshot it, or pull it up on your phone if you're
          ever stopped.
        </p>

        <h3>The evidence, in one place</h3>
        <ul class="evidence-list">
          <li><a href="https://twitter.com/MoHFW_INDIA/status/1174325122993278977?t=6X-kwOEHGvSL3ndHKTj9uQ&s=19" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Union Health Ministry's own tweet</a> (2019) stating use and possession are not banned</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.mha.gov.in/sites/default/files/CSDiv_ecigerete_12122019.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Union Home Ministry circular</a> — see point C — confirming personal use and possession is not an offence</li>
          <li><a href="https://twitter.com/vapeindia/status/1200639227425366017?t=UZhVQ7iMF4hDjVOPfSiiZw&s=19" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Video: then-Health Minister Dr Harsh Vardhan</a> assuring Parliament that use and possession would not be criminalised, during the ENDS bill debate</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.instagram.com/reel/DZ7MCllo4KJ/?igsh=MXc2aHZmMW53Zm5lMg==" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A senior advocate's legal opinion</a> on why possession isn't an offence under PECA</li>
          <li><a href="https://x.com/vapeindia/status/1848254084572172776?s=20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AVI's full commentary</a> walking through the Health Ministry's contradictory position</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          This isn't a theoretical risk. Officers exploiting the confusion
          around e-cigarettes for extortion is a documented pattern serious
          enough that it has led to real consequences and even attracted
          impersonators: two Bangalore constables were <a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/bengaluru/2-constables-dismissed-over-extortion/articleshow/98846356.cms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dismissed for extorting a vaper</a>, and in Mumbai a <a href="https://www.freepressjournal.in/amp/mumbai/mumbai-police-arrest-fake-cop-for-demanding-50000-from-woman-smoking-e-cigarette-in-auto-2-others-held" target="_blank" rel="noopener">fake police officer was arrested</a> for demanding ₹50,000 from a woman vaping in an auto-rickshaw — a
          con that only works because the underlying "it's banned" claim
          sounds plausible.
        </p>

        <h3>If you're stopped by police over vaping</h3>
        <ol class="tips-list">
          <li>Stay calm and polite on the street — you make your case with paperwork and information, not by arguing with an officer in the moment.</li>
          <li>Ask for the officer's name, badge/service number and station. A genuine officer will give you this without hesitation.</li>
          <li>Ask exactly which law and section they believe you've violated. There isn't one — PECA's offences attach to production, sale, storage-for-sale, transport-for-sale and advertisement, not personal use or possession.</li>
          <li>Never hand over cash "to settle it" on the spot. Indian law has no provision for an on-the-spot cash fine here. That is what a bribe — or a scam — looks like, not a real penalty; see the Mumbai and Bangalore cases above.</li>
          <li>If they insist on confiscating your device or continuing to detain you, ask to be taken to the station and for a written seizure memo. A lawful seizure is documented; an unlawful one usually isn't.</li>
          <li>Pull up the <a href="https://www.mha.gov.in/sites/default/files/CSDiv_ecigerete_12122019.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Home Ministry's circular</a> on your phone if it helps — point C states plainly that personal use and possession is not a crime.</li>
          <li>Record audio or video of the interaction if you can safely do so. Simply asking "do you mind if I record this?" is often enough on its own to de-escalate an attempted shakedown — a pattern widely corroborated in vaper communities online (r/electronic_cigarette, r/india) as the single most effective de-escalation step.</li>
          <li>Note the time, location, vehicle number and any badge numbers you can see, even if the officer won't give you their name directly.</li>
          <li>Afterwards, report it — to your state's police complaints authority or DGP grievance portal, and to the Anti-Corruption Bureau if money was demanded or taken.</li>
          <li><a href="/testimonials">Tell AVI what happened</a>. We track patterns of harassment and use them in our advocacy and litigation — your report helps the next person too.</li>
        </ol>

        <p class="rights-note">
          This is AVI's understanding of the law, not formal legal advice —
          if you're facing an active legal matter, consult a lawyer. But the
          underlying facts above — what PECA's text actually says, and what
          both ministries have themselves stated — are a matter of public
          record, not opinion.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="law-body">
    <div class="law-prose">
      <h2><Icon name="leaf" /> Nicotine pouches and snus</h2>
      <p>
        Nicotine pouches and snus fall outside PECA's definition of ENDS — they
        involve no vapour, aerosol or heating element. That doesn't mean they're
        lawfully available, though — they're blocked by a combination of two
        separate mechanisms, neither of which is a clean, single-line ban the
        way PECA is for e-cigarettes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No domestic sale pathway.</strong> As oral products intended
          for human consumption, they fall within the Food Safety and Standards
          Authority of India's (FSSAI) regulatory ambit under the Food Safety
          and Standards Act, 2006, and FSSAI has not approved nicotine for use
          in any food product.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Import is a case-by-case discretionary call, not an outright
          ban.</strong> Nicotine pouches fall under a "restricted" (not
          prohibited) goods classification with India's customs authorities,
          meaning import requires a specific licence for which the Directorate
          General of Foreign Trade (DGFT) consults the health ministry — in
          practice resulting in refusal, but this is administrative discretion
          applied case by case, not a standing statutory prohibition the way
          PECA is.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The net effect — no lawful way to sell, and no reliable way to import —
        is similar to PECA's outcome for e-cigarettes, but the legal <em>mechanism</em>
        genuinely differs, and unlike PECA there is no single Act that names
        nicotine pouches directly. COTPA (2003) doesn't list them among
        regulated tobacco products, PECA covers only electronic/vapour devices,
        and their status under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 is unsettled. <a href="https://vajiramandravi.com/current-affairs/nicotine-pouches-in-india-legal-status-regulation-and-health-concerns/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Some commentators have argued</a> a direct Customs Act / Foreign Trade Act notification could close this
        gap cleanly in a way that hasn't yet been done.
      </p>
      <p class="callout-note">
        This is AVI's understanding of the regulatory position, not formal
        legal advice; independent verification is recommended for anyone
        making decisions based on it.
      </p>

      <h2><Icon name="heart" /> Nicotine replacement therapy (NRT), for contrast</h2>
      <p>
        Gums, patches and similar nicotine replacement products are legal in
        India, regulated as medicines under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940,
        and receive government support as cessation aids. AVI's position isn't
        that NRT should be restricted — it's that reduced-risk nicotine
        products are being held to a different, stricter standard than NRT
        despite comparable or better real-world quit outcomes; see our <a href="/science">evidence library</a> for the research behind that
        claim.
      </p>

      <h2><Icon name="document" /> What this means practically</h2>
      <p>
        This site does not sell, advertise, or facilitate access to any
        nicotine product, in India or otherwise, and does not provide
        purchasing guidance. Nothing on this site should be read as
        encouraging violation of PECA or any other applicable law. AVI's
        position is that adult smokers should have <em>legal, regulated</em>
        access to lower-risk alternatives — our advocacy is directed at
        changing the law through evidence, litigation and public debate, not
        at circumventing it.
      </p>

      <p>
        See our <a href="/litigation">litigation</a> page for the legal
        challenges AVI has organised and fought against state-level bans that
        preceded PECA, and against the aviation-security flight ban that PECA
        doesn't touch — including the case AVI is actively running today — and
        their current status.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <style>
    .eyebrow {
      font-size: 0.82rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      color: var(--gold-light);
      margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    }
    .law-hero {
      background: linear-gradient(rgba(18,19,15,0.85), rgba(18,19,15,0.93)), url('/images/backgrounds/open-sky.jpg');
      background-size: cover;
      background-position: center 60%;
      padding: 3.25rem 0 2.75rem;
    }
    .law-hero h1 { color: #fff; margin: 0; font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.6vw, 2.6rem); }

    .law-body { max-width: var(--max-w-wide); margin: 0 auto; padding: 2.75rem 1.5rem 4rem; }

    .status-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
      gap: 1.5rem;
      margin-bottom: 3rem;
    }
    .status-card {
      background: #fff;
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 1.5rem 1.5rem 1.6rem;
      border-top: 4px solid;
      box-shadow: 0 1px 3px rgba(20,25,20,0.06);
    }
    .status-card :global(svg) { width: 1.8rem; height: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 0.85rem; }
    .status-card h3 { margin: 0 0 0.35rem; font-size: 1.05rem; }
    .status-card p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.5; }
    .status-tag {
      font-weight: 700 !important;
      font-size: 0.78rem !important;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.03em;
      margin-bottom: 0.6rem !important;
    }
    .status-banned { border-color: var(--rust); }
    .status-banned :global(svg) { color: var(--rust); }
    .status-banned .status-tag { color: var(--rust-dark); }
    .status-blocked { border-color: var(--gold); }
    .status-blocked :global(svg) { color: var(--gold); }
    .status-blocked .status-tag { color: var(--gold-dark); }
    .status-legal { border-color: var(--accent); }
    .status-legal :global(svg) { color: var(--accent); }
    .status-legal .status-tag { color: var(--accent-dark); }
    @media (max-width: 780px) {
      .status-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    }

    .law-prose { max-width: var(--max-w); }
    .law-prose h2 {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.6rem;
      font-size: 1.25rem;
      margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    }
    .law-prose h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
    .law-prose h2 :global(svg) { width: 1.3rem; height: 1.3rem; color: var(--accent); flex-shrink: 0; }
    .callout-note {
      background: var(--paper-alt);
      border-left: 3px solid var(--gold);
      padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      font-style: italic;
      color: var(--muted);
    }

    /* Know your rights — deliberately loud: full-bleed, dark, bordered,
       so it reads as a distinct reference section rather than more prose. */
    .rights-section {
      background: linear-gradient(160deg, var(--rust-dark), var(--ink-black) 75%);
      color: #f7ede6;
      padding: 2.75rem 0 3rem;
      border-top: 4px solid var(--rust);
      border-bottom: 4px solid var(--rust);
    }
    .rights-content { max-width: 44rem; }
    .rights-eyebrow {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.5rem;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      color: #f6b48a;
      margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    }
    .rights-eyebrow :global(svg) { width: 1.1rem; height: 1.1rem; }
    .rights-section h2 { color: #fff; margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 1.9rem); line-height: 1.25; }
    .rights-section h3 { color: #fff; margin: 2rem 0 0.85rem; font-size: 1.1rem; }
    .rights-lede { color: #f0e3da; font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0; }
    .rights-section p { color: #f0e3da; line-height: 1.6; }
    .rights-section p a,
    .evidence-list a,
    .tips-list a { color: #ffd3b0; text-decoration: underline; }
    .rights-section p a:hover,
    .evidence-list a:hover,
    .tips-list a:hover { color: #fff; }
    .evidence-list,
    .tips-list {
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.07);
      border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.18);
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 1.25rem 1.25rem 1.25rem 2.4rem;
      margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    }
    .evidence-list li,
    .tips-list li { margin-bottom: 0.65rem; line-height: 1.55; }
    .evidence-list li:last-child,
    .tips-list li:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .rights-note {
      font-size: 0.88rem;
      font-style: italic;
      color: #ddccc1;
      border-left: 3px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.3);
      padding-left: 1rem;
      margin-top: 1.5rem;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
